    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Dates to the early 20th century as a localized expansion of Crazy Eights. It gained popularity in New Zealand, Australia, the UK, and parts of the United States as a faster, more aggressive social game.',
      originCountries: [
        'United States',
        'New Zealand',
        'Australia',
        'United Kingdom',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1930s: Mau-Mau and Crazy Eights rules consolidate in Europe and America.',
        "1960s: 'Last One' becomes a standard schoolyard and pub game in New Zealand and Australia.",
        '1971: The commercial game UNO is released, which is essentially a streamlined, proprietary version of Last One/Crazy Eights.',
        '1980s: Traced back to Walla Walla prison in Washington State, USA, according to some sources.',
        'Present: Remains a common generic card game known by various regional names worldwide.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game evolved to have 'stackable' penalties, where multiple 2s played in sequence can force a player to draw 4, 6, or more cards. The 'Last Card' announcement rule was formalized to prevent players from sneaking out a win.",
      cultural: "In New Zealand, it is almost exclusively known as 'Last Card' and is a staple of school lunch breaks and family gatherings. The game serves as an introduction to card games for many children due to its simple rules and exciting special cards.",
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '2-6 players (4-5 is optimal).',
      deck: "Standard 52-card deck + 2 Jokers. Jokers are typically 'Draw 5' or 'Draw 6' penalty cards.",
      equipment: 'Standard deck and a flat surface.',
      dealing: "Each player receives 7 cards (or 4-9 cards at dealer's choice). One card is turned up to start the discard pile. Remaining cards form the stock.",
    },
